// app:app — the runtime's engine room.
//
// **`app` is a runtime, not a library.** That is the sentence the rest of this
// file is written against, and it is the sentence this file used to get wrong.
// It described a library a program drives: the program brought `app:draw` up,
// the program made a canvas, the program rendered it, the program called
// `input.clear_edges()` at the right moment, the program decided which
// allocator everything landed in. Every one of those is now the runtime's, and
// what is left for a program to write is the part that is actually the
// program's.
//
// ## What a runtime is for, and who it is not for
//
// It is **opinionated**, and that is the point rather than an apology. There is
// one frame shape, one root canvas, one persistent allocator, one per-frame
// temp arena, one input barrier and one place each of them happens. A program
// that wants to prototype — to get a window, a canvas and a loop and then spend its
// attention on the thing it is actually making — writes four lines and gets all
// of it:
//
//     import app "app:graphical"
//     import "app:draw"
//
//     !void ready():
//         draw.Item bg = draw.canvas_item_create(app.canvas())
//         draw.rect(bg, (0.0, 0.0, 800.0, 600.0), (26, 30, 38, 255))
//
//     !i32 main():
//         try app.init(800, 600, "sketch")
//         app.hook(ready: ready)
//         try app.run() else code:
//             return code
//         return 0
//
// There is no `draw.init` in that program, no `canvas_create`, no
// `canvas_render`, no `clear_edges` and no allocator written anywhere — and it
// draws, every frame, for as long as the window is open. That is what "a
// runtime is packaged for you" means here.
//
// **What the runtime does not take over is a font.** That was the obvious next
// thing to package and it is deliberately absent; the argument, with the bytes
// it would have cost every program, is at `app:graphical`'s `canvas()`.
//
// **What a program gives up is control over all six of those decisions.** The
// root canvas is the runtime's and is rendered on the runtime's schedule. The
// ambient allocator inside every hook is the runtime's, and which of its two
// arenas it is, is the runtime's too. The input edges are
// cleared on the runtime's clock. The pass is opened and closed by the runtime
// and a program never sees the handle. If any of that is the thing you are
// building — a custom engine, a second render target, a frame graph, an
// allocator strategy per subsystem, a loop that is not one-frame-at-a-time —
// **this is the wrong package**, and the repair is not to fight it: import
// `app:display`, `app:draw`, `app:input` and `core:jobs` directly and write the
// twenty lines this package is. They are all public, none of them requires this
// one, and `app:graphical`'s `run_loop` is the worked example of driving them
// by hand.
//
// ## What is still in this file, and what is not
//
// The app is a singleton — one window, one loop, one app — so nothing is
// threaded on a `^App`: the state lives here in package scope and is reached
// through accessors, the way `#default` is ambient. The lifecycle hooks take no
// app and no allocator, and **all four are failable**:
//
//     !void ready()               // once, after the surface is up
//     !void process(f32 dt)       // once per frame, outside the pass
//     !void draw()                // once per drawn frame, inside the pass
//     !void shutdown()            // once, on teardown
//
// A program imports a *facade* (`app:headless`, `app:graphical`) to install a
// backend on the singleton, assigns its hooks — one at a time with
// `on_ready`/`on_process`/`on_draw`/`on_shutdown`, or all four in one call with
// `hook` — and calls `run`. One backend at a time, one process-wide app, and the
// hooks are the only entry points a program writes.
//
// **The facade is the front door, and this package is the room behind it.** A
// program binds its facade under the name `app` (`import app "app:graphical"`)
// and reaches `init`, `hook`, `run`, `quit`, `canvas` and the two allocators
// through it; every one of those but `init`, `run` and `canvas` is a forward to
// a name declared here.
//
// **This package imports no backend, no window and no drawing.** That is not
// tidiness, it is what makes the escape hatch above real: a program that must
// not link sokol installs a `Backend` of its own and gets the loop, the
// allocators, the job pool and the frame order with nothing else attached. The
// five *services* a runtime drives — bringing a drawing surface up, tearing it
// down, rendering the root canvas, delivering what other threads queued for
// the frame, and ending the input frame — are therefore **slots on the
// `Backend` vtable** rather than calls written here.
//
// One of the four is forced and the other three follow it. `app:input` imports
// *this* package (it asks `supports(Capability.Input)` before it drains), so
// this package calling `input.clear_edges()` is `ERR0302` — the import graph is
// acyclic and that edge is the cycle. A vtable slot is the way out, and once
// there is one mechanism for reaching a package this file may not import, using
// a second mechanism for the three that it merely *should* not import would be
// two answers to one question. So `surface_up`, `surface_down`, `render`,
// `end_input` and `deliver` are all slots, all nil-checked, and a backend that
// fills none of them still runs.
package app

import "core:mem/arena"
import "core:mem/libc"
import "core:jobs"
import "core:threads"

// ── role (an inert build seat) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
// The composite client/server split is a build constant the driver swaps with
// `-D ROLE=server` / `-D ROLE=client`. Inert at `"standalone"`; the
// networking layer is what reads it in a `when`.
@const string8 ROLE = "standalone"

// ── lifecycle hooks ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The only hooks. `process` and `draw` are the safe region for touching the
// work pool; outside ready/process/draw/shutdown, races may occur.
//
// ## All four are failable, and the old `void()` shapes are gone
//
// A hook may `fail`, and the code it fails with becomes `run`'s failure, which
// becomes `main`'s, which becomes the process exit status. That is the whole
// chain and it is written out at `run` below.
//
// **A `void()` function can no longer fill one of these**, and there is no
// spelling that lets it. It is `ERR0504` at the `on_*` or `hook` call — *the
// place assigned to is ReadyFn = !void(), and this is void()* — naming both
// shapes, on the line that sets the hook, at compile time. The repair is one
// character: put a `!` on the hook's return type. A hook that never fails is a
// `!void` function containing no `fail`, which compiles and costs nothing.
//
// **Keeping the old shapes working was attempted and abandoned, and here is the
// arithmetic.** Two types per hook — `ReadyFn` and a failable twin — means two
// package globals per hook, two setters per hook, and a `hook` with eight
// parameters instead of four, of which six are `void()` or `!void()` and so
// cannot be told apart by any check. It also means two frame shapes again: a
// `tick` that calls one slot or the other, and every sentence in this file
// about what happens when a hook fails hedged with *"if it is the failable
// one"*. **This file has been here before.** The section below on `on_draw`
// records what carrying two frame shapes cost the last time and why they were
// collapsed; the same argument applies with the same force, and the cost of the
// flag day is measured the same way: this tree has seven programs with hooks,
// twenty-one hook declarations between them, and the edit to each is one
// character.
//
// ## `on_draw`, and where the render pass goes (C4a)
//
// **`draw` is the hook that has a render pass open; `process` is the hook that
// does not** — on every frame of every program, with no condition anywhere that
// selects otherwise. `tick` runs `process` first, outside the pass, then
// brackets the draw hook with the backend's `begin_frame`/`end_frame`. So a
// frame's simulation, its input decode, its buffer mutation and its 3.6 MB
// `fwrite` all happen before `sg_begin_pass`, and only the drawing happens
// between the pass calls.
//
// Before this there was one hook and it ran inside the pass, because the pass
// bracket *is* the frame bracket and there was nowhere else to put it. That was
// harmless while nothing in the tree touched the GPU and wrong the moment a
// real client existed: `example/edit` decoded input, mutated a rope and wrote a
// file to disk with a swapchain pass open.
//
// ## A draw hook is optional now, and most programs have none
//
// **The runtime renders its own root canvas**, so the picture a program
// recorded in `ready` is painted on every frame with no draw hook anywhere. See
// `render` in the frame order at `tick`. What a draw hook is *for* now is the
// part of a picture that changes: re-recording an item whose contents moved
// this frame, or issuing drawing of a program's own that the canvas model does
// not cover.
//
// ## The second frame shape is retired, and this is what it cost to keep
//
// **There used to be two shapes, chosen by `g_draw == nil`.** With no draw hook
// the bracket wrapped `process` — `begin_frame` → `process` → `end_frame` — and
// with one it wrapped `draw`. It was written that way so that C4a would not be
// a flag day: a program that had not yet separated its drawing kept the frame
// it had, byte for byte, and migrated when it chose to.
//
// The nil check was cheap. Carrying the two shapes was not. **A frame is the
// one thing in a framework every other sentence is written against**, and with
// two of them every such sentence had to be written twice or hedged once:
// `set_redraw_on_demand` carried a documented do-nothing case, `app:display`'s
// `drawing()` answered differently about the *same* hook depending on a
// program's hook table, and the migration this bought was a migration nobody in
// the tree ever had to make — which is the measurement that settled it.
//
// **What it breaks, outside this tree, is a program that draws inside `process`
// and sets no draw hook.** That program is now wrong. It is wrong *loudly*, on
// frame one, by name: its drawing calls issue GPU commands with no pass open,
// which `sokol_gfx`'s validation layer refuses by name, and which `app:draw`'s
// own primitives refuse ahead of even that, because they ask
// `display.drawing()` and `#panic` outside the pass. It does not get a blank
// window, a silently dropped frame or a wrong picture — the three endings a
// silent change would have given it. The repair is one line: move the drawing
// into a function of its own and hand it to `on_draw`, or to
// `hook(draw: draw_frame)`.
//
// That is the trade, stated plainly: one flag day, refused by name on the first
// frame, against a second frame shape maintained forever.
type ReadyFn:    !void()
type ProcessFn:  !void(f32)
type DrawFn:     !void()
type ShutdownFn: !void()

private ReadyFn    g_ready
private ProcessFn  g_process
private DrawFn     g_draw
private ShutdownFn g_shutdown

// Assign the lifecycle hooks. Order does not matter; call before `run`.
void on_ready(ReadyFn f):       g_ready = f
void on_process(ProcessFn f):   g_process = f
void on_shutdown(ShutdownFn f): g_shutdown = f

// Assign the draw hook — see the section above. `process` is outside the render
// pass whether or not this is set, and the runtime renders its root canvas
// whether or not this is set, so a program that names no draw hook still gets a
// picture. This is for the part of the picture that changes.
void on_draw(DrawFn f):         g_draw = f

// ── `hook`: four setters in one call ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ## Write it all-named. The language cannot make you, and here is why
//
// **`app.hook(ready: ready, process: process, draw: draw_frame,
// shutdown: shutdown)`** — every argument named, every time. Not as a style
// rule. As the only check there is.
//
// `ReadyFn`, `DrawFn` and `ShutdownFn` are each `!void()`, and in this language
// a slot's name is a *view* over a shape rather than part of the shape's
// identity, so those three declarations are **one type** and one arena entry.
// There is nothing left for a check to compare. `hook(draw_frame, ready)` —
// first and third swapped, written positionally and short — is exactly as
// well-typed as the call its author meant, and no diagnostic exists that could
// be raised for it. A named call has no positions to get wrong: `ERR0602`
// refuses a name the callee did not declare and `ERR0603` refuses a slot filled
// twice, so a typo is a refusal instead of a silent reordering.
//
// **`distinct` would catch the swap, and it was considered and rejected.**
// Tagging the three makes `hook(draw_frame, ready)` a `ERR0504` at the
// argument, naming both shapes — precisely the refusal wanted. It also refuses
// `on_ready(ready)`, `on_draw(draw_frame)` and every existing call site in this
// tree and outside it, because a plain `!void()` cannot fill a tagged slot
// without a conversion written at each one, and a nominal wrapper round every
// hook in every program is a tax on everybody to protect the one call that is
// written short. Keeping the old surface working was decided first. So the tag
// is not taken, the three stay one type, and the names are the protection.
//
// ## What a swapped positional call does at run time
//
// Nothing is refused and nothing is nil, so the program runs — which is why the
// symptom is worth writing down. The giveaway is always **frequency**: a hook
// running once that should run per frame, or per frame that should run once.
//
//   * `draw_frame` installed as `ready` runs **once**, before the first frame
//     and outside any pass. Under `app:draw` that is a `#panic` out of
//     `display.drawing()`; under raw `sokol_gfx` it is a named validation
//     refusal. This one is loud.
//   * `ready` installed as `draw` runs **every frame, inside the pass**. Its
//     one-time setup is done again per frame — an atlas rebuilt, a canvas
//     recreated, an allocation that grows without bound — and nothing is ever
//     drawn, because the thing that draws was never installed. This one is
//     quiet, and it is the one to look for: a memory graph that climbs and a
//     window that stays at the clear colour.
//   * `shutdown` installed as `draw` prints its end-of-run report on every
//     frame, which is the easiest of the three to spot from a terminal.
//
// **`process` is the one slot a swap cannot reach.** `ProcessFn` takes an `f32`
// and the other three take nothing, so a shape check has something to say about
// it and `ERR0504` catches it — that one, and only that one.
//
// ## The defaults are functions because `nil` cannot be one
//
// A default folds at the declaration and `nil` is not a compile-time constant
// (`ERR0901`), so "unset" cannot be spelled `nil` here. It is spelled as a
// named do-nothing function instead, and `hook` **compares** each argument
// against that function rather than calling it: an argument that *is* the
// sentinel is an omitted parameter, and an omitted parameter leaves that hook
// exactly as it was. That is what makes `hook(ready: r)` followed by
// `hook(draw: d)` two calls rather than the second undoing the first, and it is
// what keeps `g_draw` genuinely nil for a program that never named a draw hook
// — see below for why that still matters.
//
// **Two sentinels, one per arity, and not four.** The comparison rests on C's
// promise that two different functions have two different addresses (C11
// 6.5.9), and the one thing that breaks that promise is identical-code folding
// at link time, which is off by default in every linker this tree builds with.
// Four empty `!void()` bodies would be four candidates for folding into one;
// two functions whose *shapes* differ cannot be folded together at all. So this
// is the arrangement that leans on the promise least, which is the only reason
// the count is two rather than one per slot.
//
// **Both are `private`, and a defaulted call from another package still works**
// — measured: `app.hook(ready: r)` written in a `main` package compiles with
// these unexported. A default is call-site sugar rather than part of the type,
// but the sugar is expanded where the signature is known and not by pasting the
// name into the caller, so "unset" is a spelling nobody outside this file can
// write down. That is the point of it being private: a program that wants a
// hook unset writes `on_draw(nil)`, and there is no public name for the
// sentinel that could be handed in and then quietly skipped.
//
// The two carry the `!` for one reason and it is not that either of them can
// fail: a sentinel has to *be* the hook type to be comparable with one, and the
// hook type has the `!`.
private !void nothing():               return
private !void nothing_process(f32 dt): _ = dt

// ## And what "is a draw hook set" is for now
//
// Under the two-shape frame this question **chose a frame shape**, and getting
// it wrong put the pass in the wrong place. Under one shape it does not choose
// anything: the bracket runs either way. All it decides is whether there is
// something to call *between* `begin_frame` and the runtime's own render — so a
// wrong answer now costs one null call or one skipped one, and never a
// misplaced pass.
//
// It is still asked honestly rather than allowed to rot, because `tick` has to
// know whether to call through `g_draw` and a nil call is undefined behaviour
// rather than a no-op. The answer stays `g_draw != nil`: `hook` skips the slot
// whose argument is the sentinel, so a program that never named a draw hook
// leaves `g_draw` nil, exactly as it would have with the four setters. **The
// sentinel is never stored.** The alternative — store `nothing` and call it
// every frame — would have made the question unanswerable (nil and "defaulted"
// would be indistinguishable) and bought an indirect call per frame for it.
//
// Assign any subset of the four hooks. An omitted parameter leaves that hook
// alone. Order does not matter; call before `run`.
void hook(ReadyFn    ready    = nothing,
          ProcessFn  process  = nothing_process,
          DrawFn     draw     = nothing,
          ShutdownFn shutdown = nothing):
    if ready != nothing:
        g_ready = ready
    if process != nothing_process:
        g_process = process
    if draw != nothing:
        g_draw = draw
    if shutdown != nothing:
        g_shutdown = shutdown

// ── conditional frames (E7) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **sokol cannot idle, and this does not pretend otherwise.** `_sapp_linux_run`
// is a hard loop — `XPending` (which does not block), drain, frame callback,
// `XFlush`, repeat — with no `select`, no `XNextEvent` wait and no
// wait-for-event mode of any kind; the only thing that ever blocks is
// `glXSwapBuffers` under vsync, and that stops blocking the moment the driver
// is software Mesa or the window is unmapped. There is no `sapp_desc` field
// that changes this. So *"wake only when an event arrives"* is not on offer and
// nothing below claims it.
//
// What is on offer is not doing the **work**. A frame that changes nothing
// costs a render pass, a client's whole paint walk, a vertex upload and a
// buffer swap; skipping those is most of the cost of an idle text editor, and
// `app:graphical` adds a short nap on a skipped frame so the loop yields its
// core instead of spinning it. What remains is a cheap poll at a rate the
// backend chooses.
//
// **Off by default.** With `set_redraw_on_demand(false)` — the default — every
// frame draws and nothing here has any effect. It is opt-in because sokol
// reports no expose or damage event, so nothing can tell a program that its
// window was occluded and needs repainting; on a compositing desktop the
// compositor holds those pixels and it does not matter, and without one an
// idle window can show stale content until something asks for a frame. A
// program that turns this on is stating that it knows when its content
// changes.
//
// **It skips the bracket and it never skips `process`.** `process` drains the
// input queue and advances the clock, so it runs on every frame whatever this
// is set to; what a skipped frame skips is `begin_frame`, the draw hook, the
// runtime's render and `end_frame`. It does **not** skip the input barrier —
// see `end_input` in the frame order at `tick`, which is outside the bracket
// for exactly that reason.
private bool g_on_demand
private bool g_redraw = true
private bool g_drew

// Ask for the next frame to be drawn. Cheap, idempotent, and safe to call from
// anywhere — a program calls it from wherever its state changes rather than
// reasoning about frames.
void request_redraw():
    g_redraw = true

// Draw every frame (`false`, the default) or only after `request_redraw`
// (`true`). Turning it on requests one frame immediately, so a program that
// enables it in `ready` paints once before it goes quiet.
void set_redraw_on_demand(bool on):
    g_on_demand = on
    if on:
        g_redraw = true

// Whether the next `tick` will open the pass. Always true while on-demand
// redrawing is off.
bool redraw_pending():
    return !g_on_demand || g_redraw

// Whether the **last** `tick` opened the render-pass bracket — which is the
// same question as whether it presented a frame, and is what a backend asks to
// decide whether to nap. It is not quite "ran the draw hook": a program with no
// draw hook still brackets, still clears, still has its root canvas rendered
// and still presents, and still answers true.
bool drew():
    return g_drew

// ── capabilities ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// What a backend supports, and **it must be what the backend can actually do**:
// `app:graphical` spent a milestone advertising all six while `run_loop` was a
// `#panic`, which made `supports` worth nothing to ask. `app:headless`
// advertises `Threads` and nothing else; `app:graphical` advertises the five it
// honours — `VectorDraw` among them now that it is the facade that brings
// `app:draw` up — and not `Audio`, because it has no audio device.
//
// A capability is a **query**, not a gate: `supports` is the whole surface.
// There is no `require`-style refusal — a subsystem that cannot service a call
// answers it emptily (`input.poll` returns 0 on a backend with no `Input`, so
// polling is safe to write unguarded on every backend) and a program that wants
// to behave differently asks `supports` first.
enum u32 Capability: (
    Input      = 1,
    VectorDraw = 2,
    Windowing  = 4,
    Audio      = 8,
    Threads    = 16,
    Clipboard  = 32,
)

distinct type Caps: u32

// Build a capability set from a list of capabilities.
Caps caps(..Capability cs):
    u32 v = 0
    for cp in cs:
        v = v | u32(cp)
    return Caps(v)

// ── the backend vtable ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A backend is its own state plus a table of function references, all nullary and
// named only in terms of the singleton — nothing substrate-specific leaks up here.
// `run` owns the loop and calls the frame steps; `begin_frame`/`end_frame` bracket
// the draw hook and the runtime's render.
//
// ## The five service slots, and why they are slots
//
// `surface_up`, `surface_down`, `render`, `end_input` and `deliver` are the
// packages this
// runtime *drives* — `app:draw` and `app:input` — reached through the one seat a
// backend already fills. The argument for a slot rather than an import is in the
// package comment: `app:input` imports this package, so an import back is
// `ERR0302`, and having reached for a vtable once it would be two answers to one
// question to reach for anything else for the other three.
//
//   surface_up    bring the drawing surface up. Called **once**, inside
//                 `startup`, after the job pool and **before** `ready`, so the
//                 program's `ready` has a canvas to record into. The one
//                 failable slot: `draw.init` answers false when there is no
//                 shader for the live `sokol_gfx` backend or an allocation
//                 refused, and that is a condition a program has to hear about.
//   surface_down  tear it down. Called inside `teardown`, **after** `shutdown`,
//                 so a program's own release runs while its canvas is still
//                 valid.
//   render        render the runtime's root canvas. Called inside the pass,
//                 **after** the draw hook — the ordering is argued at `tick`.
//   end_input     end the input frame (`input.clear_edges`). Called last in
//                 `tick`, outside the bracket, on every frame.
//   deliver       take what was queued for this frame (`input.deliver`, which
//                 drains `app:input`'s signal). Called first in `tick`, before
//                 `process`, on every frame — the one point the frame drains.
//
// **Every one is nil-checked and a backend may fill none of them.** A backend
// with no drawing surface leaves the first three nil and loses nothing; a
// backend with no event source leaves the last two nil. That is what makes this
// package's claim to import no backend, no window and no drawing a true one.
//
// **Ten slots, and the swap hazard is real.** Seven of them are `void()` and are
// therefore one type, so a `Backend` literal written in the wrong order is not
// checkable — the same hazard `hook` has and for the same reason. There is no
// named form for a tuple literal to lean on here, so the protection is that a
// `Backend` is constructed in exactly three places in this tree (`app:graphical`,
// `app:headless`, and this package's own test) and each one is written out on
// four lines with the slot each argument fills sitting directly above it. The
// newest slot goes last, so no existing position moved when it arrived.
type Backend: (
    rawptr data,
    void() run,
    void() begin_frame,
    void() end_frame,
    Caps caps,
    !void() surface_up,
    void() surface_down,
    void() render,
    void() end_input,
    void() deliver,
)

private Backend g_backend
private bool    g_backend_set

// A facade installs its backend on the singleton before `run`.
void set_backend(Backend b):
    g_backend = b
    g_backend_set = true

// The backend's own opaque state, for a facade that needs it. Nil until set.
rawptr backend_data():
    return g_backend.data

// ── stopping, and the two ways it happens ───────────────────────────────────
//
// **A program stops the loop with `quit()`. The runtime stops it with a
// failure, and the two are deliberately different flags.**
//
// `g_quitting` is the program's and is **set-only** — there is no `unquit`, and
// adding one to suit a caller would be the caller steering the design. So a run
// that ends in `quit()` ends every later run in the same process too: the
// loop's own condition refuses to enter. That is a known property and two tests
// in this tree are ordered around it.
//
// `g_failed` is the runtime's and is **cleared by `run`**, so a run that ended
// in a hook's failure does not poison the next one. The distinction is not new:
// `app:headless`'s frame budget already ends its loop through a local rather
// than through `app.quit()`, for exactly this reason, and a failure is the same
// kind of ending — the runtime's decision about its own loop, not the
// program's.
private bool g_quitting
private bool g_failed
private i32  g_fail_code

// Ask the loop to stop after this frame.
void quit():
    g_quitting = true

// A backend's loop polls this to know when to stop. True when the program asked
// **or** when a hook failed — a backend needs no second condition and no
// knowledge that failures exist.
bool quitting():
    return g_quitting || g_failed

// Record a failure raised by a hook or a service and stop the loop.
//
// **First code wins.** A failing `process` sets the code; the `shutdown` that
// runs on the way out may fail too, and reporting the second would hand the
// program the consequence instead of the cause.
private void note_failure(i32 code):
    if !g_failed:
        g_failed = true
        g_fail_code = code

// Whether the last `run` ended in a failure, and the code it ended with. `0`
// when nothing failed. A program does not normally ask — `run` fails with the
// code and `main` answers it — but a test does.
bool failed():
    return g_failed

i32 fail_code():
    return g_fail_code

// ── capability queries ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool supports(Capability cp):
    return (u32(g_backend.caps) & u32(cp)) != 0

// **`require(Capability, string)` was here and is deleted.** It `#panic`ed on a
// missing capability and was written for "a subsystem calls this at its entry".
// It was called from nowhere in the tree, and the reason is not that nobody got
// round to it: there is exactly one capability-gated entry point in the whole
// collection — `input.poll` — and it deliberately does the opposite, returning
// 0 so that the same program runs headless and windowed with no branch in it.
// That is the pattern this framework actually wants, and a `#panic` helper
// sitting beside `supports` argued for the other one while honouring neither.
// Restoring it costs five lines over `supports` if a subsystem is ever written
// that genuinely has no empty answer to give.

// ── the program's own state pointer (optional) ──────────────────────────────
// In the singleton model a program usually keeps its state in its own package
// scope; this is here for a library that must reach it through the app.
private rawptr g_user
void set_user(rawptr p): g_user = p
rawptr user():           return g_user

// ── the two allocators ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **Two arenas, and which one is ambient depends on how often the hook runs.**
// That is the whole rule and it is worth learning as one sentence:
//
//     a hook that runs ONCE allocates for the life of the program
//     a hook that runs EVERY FRAME allocates for the life of the frame
//
//     ready, shutdown         →  using allocator()   the persistent arena
//     process, draw           →  using temp()        the app's temp arena,
//                                                    rolled back every frame
//
// The one allocator rule is *don't thread allocators as parameters; change the
// ambient with `using`* — so the runtime opens the `using` and a program's
// `#make`, `#new`, `#copy` and `#format` land in the right arena with nothing
// written anywhere.
//
// ## Why per-frame is the default inside a per-frame hook
//
// **Because in a frame loop, per-frame allocation is the common case, and a
// runtime should make the common case free and persistence the thing you write
// down.** A `process` that formats a label, builds a candidate list, collects
// contacts, sorts a visible set, measures a string — every one of those is
// storage for this frame and nothing else, and every one of them is something
// an author would otherwise have to remember to free, or to allocate through a
// name they have to go and learn first. Under this arrangement they write
// `#make` and it costs a pointer bump and is gone at the end of the tick.
//
// The other direction was written here for one milestone and it was the wrong
// way round. It said the temp arena must never be ambient, because *"an
// allocation that landed in it silently by way of being the default would be a
// live reference to freed bytes one frame later"*. The hazard in that sentence
// is real and is **not** retracted below — what was wrong was the conclusion.
// It priced the rare mistake at the cost of making the common case ceremony, in
// a package whose entire reason to exist is that the common case should be
// free.
//
// ## The hazard, stated plainly, because it is accepted rather than avoided
//
// **A `#make` inside `process` that you store somewhere that outlives the frame
// is a dangling reference on the next frame.** Nothing reports it. The bytes are
// not freed to the heap — the arena keeps its blocks — so it is not a `free`
// you can catch; the storage is handed to the *next* frame's allocations and
// your reference quietly starts reading somebody else's data.
//
//     private ref []Enemy g_spawned            // package state: outlives the frame
//
//     !void process(f32 dt):
//         g_spawned = #make([]Enemy, n)        // WRONG — gone at the end of this tick
//
// **What you write instead is one line, and it is visible where it matters.**
// The precedence is *explicit argument > `using` > ambient*, so either of the
// two upper levels takes it back:
//
//     !void process(f32 dt):
//         using app.allocator():               // the whole suite is persistent
//             g_spawned = #make([]Enemy, n)
//
//     !void process(f32 dt):
//         g_spawned = #make([]Enemy, n, app.allocator())    // just this one
//
// An author who keeps a lot of persistent state in `process` is encouraged to
// hold an allocator of their own — their own arena, their own pool — and open a
// `using` of it; the runtime's persistent allocator is available for that and is
// not special.
//
// ## Checkpoints, for the frame that allocates a lot and then stops
//
// The temp arena is rolled back once per frame, so its high-water mark is the
// largest single frame the program ever has — and it keeps those blocks for the
// rest of the run. One frame that builds a 40 MB intermediate makes the process
// 40 MB resident for ever. `enter_temp` / `close_temp` is how an author takes
// that back inside the frame:
//
//     using app.enter_temp():                  // opens a checkpoint AND installs it
//         ref []Candidate c = #make([]Candidate, 500000)
//         choose(c)
//     app.close_temp()                         // rolls the arena back to here
//
// See the temp section below for the whole surface.
//
// ## What `app:draw` does about all this, which is nothing
//
// **`app:draw` mints its command tapes from the allocator it captured at
// `draw.init`, not from the ambient at record time.** So `draw.rect(item, …)`
// inside a `draw` hook records into storage that belongs to the drawing server
// and outlives the frame exactly as the retained model requires — the ambient
// being the temp arena there changes nothing about it. `draw.init` is called
// from `surface_up`, which is inside `startup`, which is under the *persistent*
// allocator; that is what makes the captured allocator the right one.
//
// That is also the reason `draw` is grouped with `process` and not with
// `ready`. A draw hook's own allocations — a `#format`ed label, a temporary
// point array, a measured run — are per-frame by nature, and the one thing in a
// draw hook that has to outlive the frame does not go through the ambient at
// all.
//
// `temp`       — the per-frame arena, rolled back every tick, ambient inside
//                `process` and `draw`. Allocate freely, never free.
// `allocator`  — the persistent, runtime-owned home for state that outlives a
//                frame; ambient inside `ready` and `shutdown`; backed by
//                core:mem/libc by default, swappable before `run`.
private (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data)   g_persistent
private bool        g_persistent_ready
private bool        g_running

// The persistent allocator — and the ambient `#default` inside `ready` and
// `shutdown`.
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) allocator():
    if !g_persistent_ready:
        g_persistent = libc.allocator()
        g_persistent_ready = true
    return g_persistent

// Swap the persistent allocator. **Before `run`, and this now refuses
// afterwards.**
//
// ## What changed, and why the refusal rather than a quiet swap
//
// The old comment said *"typically once, inside `ready`"*, and under a library
// that a program drove, that was harmless: nothing read `allocator()` but the
// program itself, so the swap took effect at the next call and nobody could
// tell the difference.
//
// It is not harmless now. The runtime opens a `using allocator()` around each
// of `startup`, `tick` and `teardown`, and a `using` binds the value it is
// given at the top of its suite. A swap inside `ready` therefore leaves the
// whole of `startup` — the root canvas, every item a program recorded, every
// allocation `app:draw` made bringing itself up — in the **old** allocator, and
// puts every later frame in the new one. Nothing dangles, both allocators are
// live, and that is precisely the problem: the runtime's one promise is *your
// allocations land in the runtime's allocator*, and after a mid-run swap that
// sentence has two answers depending on which frame asked.
//
// **A `#panic` and not a `!`**, on the same grounds as `run`'s two panics: this
// is a program calling a setup call after setup, which is a mistake with no
// recovery and no caller to report to. A `!` would put a `try` on every correct
// call site to carry a code that means *"you wrote this line in the wrong
// place"*, which is a compile-time-shaped problem wearing a run-time answer.
//
// **The refusal is cheap and it is exact.** `g_running` goes true in `run`
// before the loop is entered and false when it returns, and the first read of
// `allocator()` is the `using` in `startup`, which is inside the loop. So the
// line the panic refuses is exactly the line that would have been half-applied.
void set_allocator((rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) a):
    if g_running:
        #panic("app.set_allocator: the runtime is already running and has installed its allocator — call set_allocator before run(), not from a hook")
    g_persistent = a
    g_persistent_ready = true

// ── the app's temp arena ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// ## It is the app's own, and `core:runtime`'s scratch is left alone
//
// **This package does not call `core:runtime` and must not start.** Not
// `init_scratch`, not `enter_temp`, not `reset_scratch`, not `destroy_scratch`
// — checked across the whole `app:` collection and there is not one call to
// that package anywhere in it.
//
// The reason is ownership. `core:runtime`'s scratch is **the author's**: one
// arena per thread, the main thread's being the one a program configures near
// the top of `main` and uses for
// its own temporaries, at its own depths, with its own reset points. A runtime
// that quietly took a frame-sized checkpoint on it every tick would be sharing
// a LIFO stack with the person holding the other end of it — `reset_scratch`
// from a program would be refused (or deferred by `TEMP_GUARD`) because a scope
// the *runtime* opened is still live, `temp_depth()` would never read what the
// author expects, and the high-water mark an author is watching would be the
// runtime's frame mixed with their own. So `app` brings its own arena and
// `runtime.temp()` keeps meaning exactly what it meant before this package
// existed.
//
// The cost of that is this section: about forty lines that look like
// `core:runtime`'s. It is deliberate that they look like it — see the naming
// note — and it is deliberate that they are fewer.
//
// ## What is mirrored, and what is deliberately not
//
// Mirrored, name for name, so that an author who knows one knows the other:
// `temp()` (the arena as an allocator, no checkpoint), `enter_temp(escaping)`
// (a checkpoint *and* the allocator, in one call, so a frame cannot be opened
// and left uninstalled), `close_temp()`, `temp_used()`, `temp_depth()`.
//
// **Not mirrored: `TEMP_GUARD`, the deferral counter, the `Checkpoint` serials
// and the hand-held `temp_open`/`temp_close` pair.** Those exist in
// `core:runtime` to police a rollback that would free storage an outer scope is
// still handing out, in an arena with no natural outer boundary. This one has
// one: **the frame is the outermost checkpoint and it rolls back
// unconditionally.** A hook that opens a temp frame and forgets to close it
// cannot leak past the end of the tick, because `tick` resets the arena and
// empties the stack whatever depth it is at — and says so, once, on the frame
// it happened. That is the whole of what the guard machinery buys here, for one
// `#expect` instead of a subsystem.
//
// **Also not mirrored: `init_scratch`.** There is nothing to configure and no
// call to remember. The arena is made on first use and destroyed in `teardown`;
// `core:mem/arena` asks its backing allocator for nothing until the first
// allocation, so a program that never allocates a temporary pays five words.
//
// ## Naming: `temp` won, and `app.scratch()` is gone
//
// This surface used to be called `app.scratch()`, and adding `enter_temp` beside
// it would have been two vocabularies for one arena. `temp` won on three counts.
// Sam's words for it are *"the app's temp arena"*. `core:runtime` already splits
// the two words and the split is meaningful there — *scratch* is the arena's
// lifetime (`init_scratch`, `reset_scratch`, `destroy_scratch`) and *temp* is a
// checkpointed region inside it (`temp()`, `enter_temp`, `close_temp`) — and
// this package has no lifetime surface at all, only regions. And `temp` is what
// the frame boundary actually does to it: it is rolled back, not freed.
//
// **`JobArgs.scratch` keeps its name and that is not an inconsistency.** It is a
// different arena — one per worker thread, handed to a job explicitly, never the
// ambient anywhere — and it is `core:jobs`' name for `core:jobs`' thing. The
// name that moved is the one whose meaning moved.
const i32 TEMP_DEPTH = 32

private arena.Arena          g_temp_arena
private bool                 g_temp_ready
private [TEMP_DEPTH]arena.Mark g_temp_marks
private i32                  g_temp_top

private (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) temp_alloc():
    if !g_temp_ready:
        g_temp_arena = arena.over(libc.allocator())
        g_temp_ready = true
    return arena.allocator(&g_temp_arena)

// The app's temp arena, as an allocator. Already the ambient `#default` inside
// `process` and `draw`, so a per-frame `#make` there needs none of this written;
// this is for reaching it from somewhere else — a helper called outside a hook,
// or an explicit `#make([]T, n, app.temp())` argument.
//
// **It opens no checkpoint.** Storage taken through this is reclaimed by the
// frame boundary and by nothing else. `enter_temp` is the one that checkpoints.
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) temp():
    return temp_alloc()

// Open a temp frame and install it: a checkpoint is taken and the arena is
// answered, in one call, so there is no way to open a frame and forget to use
// it.
//
//     using app.enter_temp():
//         ref []Candidate c = #make([]Candidate, 500000)
//         choose(c)
//     app.close_temp()
//
// Nest these freely — each frame reclaims only its own temporaries. `escaping`
// is for the one case where the *result* of the work lives in the arena and has
// to outlive this frame: it opens no rollback, so `close_temp` unwinds the
// bookkeeping and leaves the bytes for the enclosing frame — or, at the
// outermost level, for the end of the tick. Do not reach for it to keep
// ordinary temporaries alive; that only moves them up one level.
//
// **More than `TEMP_DEPTH` nested frames opens none** and reports it, rather
// than writing past the stack. A depth of 32 inside one frame of one hook is a
// missing `close_temp`, not a deep program.
(rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) enter_temp(bool escaping = false):
    bool room = g_temp_top < TEMP_DEPTH
    if !#expect(room, "app.enter_temp: more than ", TEMP_DEPTH, " nested temp frames — opening none; look for a missing close_temp"):
        return temp_alloc()
    (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) a = temp_alloc()
    arena.Mark m = (nil, nil, 0)
    if !escaping:
        m = arena.begin(&g_temp_arena)
    g_temp_marks[g_temp_top] = m
    g_temp_top = g_temp_top + 1
    return a

// Close the innermost temp frame, rolling the arena back to where it was.
// `false` when no frame is open.
//
// An escaping frame's mark names no arena, and `arena.end` on one is a no-op by
// construction — which is why there is no second array of `escaping` bits here
// the way `core:runtime` needs one: the zero `Mark` *is* the "leave the bytes"
// instruction.
bool close_temp():
    if g_temp_top == 0:
        return false
    g_temp_top = g_temp_top - 1
    arena.end(g_temp_marks[g_temp_top])
    return true

// How many bytes the temp arena is holding. `0` at the top of every frame,
// because `tick` rolls it back at the bottom of the last one.
u64 temp_used():
    if !g_temp_ready:
        return 0
    return arena.used(g_temp_arena)

// How many temp frames are open. `0` everywhere a correct program can observe
// it, since the frame boundary empties the stack.
i32 temp_depth():
    return g_temp_top

// The frame boundary: roll the whole arena back and empty the checkpoint stack.
// Called from `tick` and from nowhere else.
//
// **It does not care what depth it is at**, and that is the property that makes
// the rest of this section short. A hook that opened a temp frame and returned
// without closing it has its storage reclaimed here anyway; what it gets is one
// report naming the depth, on the frame it happened, rather than an arena that
// grows for the rest of the run.
private void temp_frame_reset():
    if !g_temp_ready:
        return
    bool balanced = g_temp_top == 0
    #expect(balanced, "app: a hook left ", g_temp_top, " temp frame(s) open at the end of the frame — every app.enter_temp() wants an app.close_temp(). The frame boundary reclaimed them anyway.")
    g_temp_top = 0
    arena.reset(&g_temp_arena)

// ── the job system ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The job surface, delegated to a core:jobs Scheduler the app owns. The engine
// is pooled when the backend advertises `Threads` (real worker threads) and
// serial otherwise (every job inline) — the same code either way. The
// scheduler is started in `startup` and stopped in `teardown`; its per-worker
// scratch arenas reset with the frame (`tick`).
//
// `JobArgs`/`JobFn`/`Handle` are re-exposed here so a program writes `app.*`. The
// arg/fn shapes are structurally identical to core:jobs' (names are not part of a
// type), so a program's `app.JobFn` reaches the pool with no conversion; `Handle`
// is nominal, so it is carried across as its underlying `i32`.
//
// **Job concurrency contract** (see core:jobs): the ambient allocator is per
// thread, so a job's bare `#make` reads its worker's `#default` — the heap —
// and its temporaries go in `using ja.scratch:`, the worker's own arena, reset
// with the frame. **The runtime's `using` does not reach a worker thread**: it
// changes the ambient on the thread that opened it, and a job runs somewhere
// else.
type JobArgs: (
    i32       worker,
    (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) scratch,
    i32       begin,
    i32       end,
    rawptr    data,
)

type JobFn: void(^JobArgs)

distinct type Handle: i32

private jobs.Scheduler g_sched
private bool           g_sched_started

// Start the scheduler once: pooled if the backend advertises `Threads`, else
// serial. Called from `startup`, after the backend (and its caps) are installed.
private void sched_start():
    if g_sched_started:
        return
    i32 nt = 0
    if supports(Capability.Threads):
        nt = threads.hardware_concurrency() - 1
        if nt < 1:
            nt = 1
    _ = jobs.start(&g_sched, nt)
    g_sched_started = true

// Submit one job. Returns a handle to wait on (`wait`); a job hands its result
// back by emitting it into a signal the program drains in `process` (see
// `core:jobs`' header).
Handle submit(JobFn fn, rawptr data):
    return Handle(i32(jobs.submit(&g_sched, fn, data)))

// Submit `fn` to run after `dep`.
Handle submit_after(JobFn fn, rawptr data, Handle dep):
    return Handle(i32(jobs.submit_after(&g_sched, fn, data, jobs.Handle(i32(dep)))))

// Fork-join over [0, n) in `grain`-sized chunks; joins before returning.
void parallel_for(i32 n, i32 grain, JobFn fn, rawptr data):
    jobs.parallel_for(&g_sched, n, grain, fn, data)

// Cooperatively wait for a handle.
void wait(Handle h):
    jobs.wait(&g_sched, jobs.Handle(i32(h)))

// ── frame steps a backend calls ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The backend owns the loop shape (sokol's `sapp_run` owns it and calls back;
// `app:headless` writes a plain `for`); these three are the fixed lifecycle it
// drives, and **none of them is failable**.
//
// ## How a hook's failure reaches `main`, through a C loop that cannot carry it
//
// This is the one genuinely awkward part of the design and it is awkward for a
// reason no amount of arranging removes: under `app:graphical` the frame loop
// is *inside* `sapp_run`. The call stack at the moment a hook fails is
//
//     main → run → sapp_run → _sapp_frame → display's frame_cb → _frame → tick → process
//                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ C
//
// and a Dado failure has no way to unwind through those C frames. Worse, the
// three callbacks it would have to unwind *to* are `display.Hooks` slots typed
// `void()` and `void(f32)` — C function pointers sokol holds — so they could
// not carry a `!` even if C could carry the failure.
//
// So the failure is **recorded and re-raised**, in three steps:
//
//   1. `startup`, `tick` and `teardown` are the public, **non-failable** entries
//      a backend calls. Each is a `try` over the failable body beside it, with
//      an `else` that hands the code to `note_failure`. The catch lives here and
//      not in each backend so that a backend cannot forget it and a third
//      backend inherits it by doing nothing.
//   2. `note_failure` stores the first code and makes `quitting()` true. The
//      backend's loop already polls `quitting()` — `app:headless` in its `for`
//      condition, `app:graphical` in `_frame`, where it calls
//      `display.request_close()` — so the loop stops by the road it already had,
//      and neither backend needed a line for this.
//   3. `run` is failable. It clears the flag, hands the loop to the backend, and
//      when the loop returns — through however many C frames — it fails with the
//      recorded code.
//
// **What this costs: a failing hook does not stop the frame it is in at the
// instruction that failed.** It stops the *frame*. The bracket is still closed
// (an unbalanced `sg_begin_pass` is a validation error and a lost swapchain
// image), the temp arena is still rolled back, the input frame is still ended,
// and only then does `tick` fail. The frame after it does nothing at all — `run_frame`
// returns on `g_failed` before it touches a hook — and the loop exits on the
// next poll. So the worst case is one extra frame's worth of nothing between
// the failure and the exit, which is the price of the pass staying balanced.
//
// **What it does not cost: nothing is lost.** The code a hook failed with is
// the code `main` gets, and `!i32 main()` makes that the process exit status
// (measured: a hook failing with 7 exits 7).

// Once, at the top of the loop: the job pool, the drawing surface, the `ready`
// hook.
//
// **Under `using allocator()` — the persistent arena — and every word of that
// is load-bearing.** `ready` is where a program builds the things that have to
// still be there on frame 900: its canvas items, its level, its tables. A
// canvas item created into a per-frame arena is the worst version of the
// dangling-reference hazard the allocator section describes, because it does
// not look like an allocation at the call site at all. `surface_up` is under
// the same `using` for the same reason one level down: `draw.init` captures the
// ambient and mints every command tape from it for the whole run.
void startup():
    try run_startup() else code:
        note_failure(code)

private !void run_startup():
    using allocator():
        sched_start()
        if g_backend.surface_up != nil:
            try g_backend.surface_up()
        if g_ready != nil:
            try g_ready()

// One frame. **One shape, for every program.**
//
//     deliver
//     · [ using temp():  process · [ begin_frame · draw · render · end_frame ] ]
//     · temp rollback · job scratch reset · end_input
//
// Step by step, and every step is here rather than in a backend:
//
//   0. **`deliver`** — the frame's one drain point. What another thread or the
//      backend's event read queued since the last frame is taken, here and
//      nowhere else in the frame, so `process` works on a snapshot that
//      nothing changes under it.
//   1. **`process`**, outside the pass, always. Simulation, input drain, file
//      writes, anything at all.
//   2. **`begin_frame`** — the backend's pass bracket opens, if this frame
//      draws. `redraw_pending()` decides, and it is unconditionally true unless
//      the program asked for on-demand redrawing.
//   3. **the `draw` hook**, inside the pass, if the program named one. This is
//      where a program *records* into the canvas tree.
//   4. **`render`** — the runtime draws its own root canvas.
//   5. **`end_frame`** — the bracket closes and the frame is presented.
//   6. **the resets** — the temp arena is rolled back and its checkpoint stack
//      emptied, then the job pool's per-worker scratch. After the bracket, so a
//      hook may allocate temporaries while drawing.
//   7. **`end_input`** — `input.clear_edges()`, outside the bracket so that a
//      skipped frame still ends its input frame.
//
// ## Why the frame drains at one point, and why it is before `process`
//
// **A signal has one consumer thread for its life, and a frame is where that
// thread already is.** Queued input is a signal (`app:input`'s
// `input_events`): the backend emits into it outside the frame barrier and
// the frame takes it. Taking it at one fixed step — before `process`, on
// every frame, drawn or skipped — is what makes the frame thread the consumer
// by construction rather than by each program's care, and what makes a
// `process` that polls until it answers 0 end however fast a producer runs:
// everything emitted after step 0 waits for the next frame's. It is the
// native counterpart of the web glue's check points, and the point a
// script's signal delivery joins when there is one.
//
// A program's own signals — a job's results, a worker's records — are drained
// by the program, in `process`, which is the same thread; the runtime has no
// way to name them and does not try.
//
// ## Steps 1 to 5 run under `using temp()`
//
// The `using` covers the whole of the frame's work and nothing else: it opens
// above `process` and closes below `end_frame`, so **`process` and `draw` — the
// two hooks that run every frame — have the temp arena as their ambient
// `#default`**, and the rollback in step 6 is outside it. The argument for
// per-frame being the default there, the dangling-reference hazard it accepts,
// and the one line an author writes when something has to outlive the frame,
// are all in the allocator section above.
//
// The three steps between the hooks are under it too and none of them cares:
// `begin_frame` and `end_frame` are pass calls, and `render` walks the canvas
// tree and grows `app:draw`'s own paint run, which is a `#resize` through the
// allocator that run already carries rather than through the ambient.
//
// **Step 6 is deliberately outside the `using`.** It is the boundary, not part
// of the frame, and it has to run at a point where nothing is still holding the
// arena as its default — including on the path where a hook failed, which is
// why it sits below the `using` rather than as its last statement.
//
// ## Why `render` comes after the draw hook and not before
//
// **Because `app:draw` is retained and recording is not drawing.** A
// `draw.rect` call appends to an item's command tape; `draw.canvas_render` is
// what walks the tree and issues. So a hook that re-records an item this frame
// and a render that ran *before* it would put this frame's change on screen
// **next** frame — a one-frame lag that is invisible in a still picture and
// reads as input latency the moment anything moves. Hook first, render second,
// and what a program recorded this frame is what it sees this frame.
//
// **What that costs is z-order for a hook that bypasses the canvas.** A draw
// hook issuing its own `sokol_gp` commands is drawn *under* the runtime's
// canvas, because its commands are recorded into the pass first. A program that
// wants to paint over the canvas does it the way the canvas model already
// provides — record into an item with a higher `z_index` — and a program that
// genuinely cannot is a program that wanted its own render order, which is the
// case the package comment sends to `app:display` and `app:draw` directly.
//
// ## Why `end_input` is the last statement of the frame
//
// `app:input`'s own prose asks for `clear_edges` at **one end** of the frame,
// and names what goes wrong otherwise: a second call inside a frame drops the
// edges set between the two and does it silently. It was the program's call and
// the owner's instruction is that it should not be, so it is the runtime's now.
//
// **The end and not the beginning, and the difference is a whole frame of
// input.** Under sokol the event callbacks for a frame run *before* that
// frame's frame callback — sokol drains its event queue, then calls the frame
// function. Clearing at the top of `tick` would therefore wipe the edges that
// this frame's events have just latched, before `process` ever looks at them: a
// key tapped between two frames would be observable by nothing. Clearing at the
// bottom puts the line between frames where the backend already puts it, so a
// frame's pushes fall entirely inside the next frame.
//
// **A program must not also call `input.clear_edges()`.** Not "should not" —
// the runtime cannot stop it and `app:input` cannot tell who is calling, so
// this is a rule rather than a refusal. What goes wrong is specific: a program
// clearing at the end of its own `process` drops the edges before its `draw`
// hook runs, so any `key_pressed` read from `draw` is false for ever, on a line
// that is correct in every other respect. `clear_keys()` — the levels, not the
// edges — is still the program's and is untouched by any of this.
void tick(f32 dt):
    try run_frame(dt) else code:
        note_failure(code)

private !void run_frame(f32 dt):
    // A frame after a failure does nothing at all. The loop has been asked to
    // stop and has not got there yet; running a program's hooks in between
    // would be running them after the runtime already gave up on them.
    if g_failed:
        return
    // Step 0: the one drain point, before anything the program runs.
    if g_backend.deliver != nil:
        g_backend.deliver()
    i32 hurt = 0
    // Steps 1–5, with the temp arena as the ambient `#default`.
    using temp():
        if g_process != nil:
            try g_process(dt) else code:
                hurt = code
        g_drew = false
        if hurt == 0:
            g_drew = redraw_pending()
            g_redraw = false
            if g_drew:
                if g_backend.begin_frame != nil:
                    g_backend.begin_frame()
                if g_draw != nil:
                    try g_draw() else code:
                        hurt = code
                // A half-recorded tree is not a picture, so a failing draw hook
                // skips the render — but never the `end_frame` below it.
                if hurt == 0 && g_backend.render != nil:
                    g_backend.render()
                if g_backend.end_frame != nil:
                    g_backend.end_frame()
    // Step 6, outside it: the boundary is not part of the frame.
    temp_frame_reset()
    if g_sched_started:
        jobs.reset_scratch(&g_sched)
    if g_backend.end_input != nil:
        g_backend.end_input()
    if hurt != 0:
        fail hurt

// Once, at the bottom of the loop: the `shutdown` hook, the drawing surface,
// the job pool, the temp arena.
//
// **Under `using allocator()`, with `ready`**, and for the matching reason: a
// `shutdown` hook is releasing things that were built persistently and may
// build one last persistent thing on the way out — a report, a saved file's
// buffer — and the temp arena is destroyed three lines later, so it is not a
// place to be allocating into. The rule stays the one sentence: a hook that
// runs once allocates for the life of the program.
//
// **Everything here runs even when the run is already failing**, and the
// `shutdown` hook is included. A release path that only runs when nothing went
// wrong is not a release path, and the frame this is unwinding from is exactly
// the one a program most wants its own cleanup to see. `note_failure` keeps the
// first code, so a `shutdown` that fails on the way out of a failing run
// reports the cause rather than the consequence.
void teardown():
    try run_teardown() else code:
        note_failure(code)

private !void run_teardown():
    using allocator():
        i32 hurt = 0
        if g_shutdown != nil:
            try g_shutdown() else code:
                hurt = code
        if g_backend.surface_down != nil:
            g_backend.surface_down()
        if g_sched_started:
            jobs.stop(&g_sched)
            g_sched_started = false
        if g_temp_ready:
            arena.destroy(&g_temp_arena)
            g_temp_ready = false
            g_temp_top = 0
        if hurt != 0:
            fail hurt

// Run the app: hand the loop to the backend, which calls the frame steps above.
//
// ## Failable now, and the old argument for `void` is what made it so
//
// This used to be `void`, and the argument was *"a `?` here would be a mark on
// a function with nothing to report"* — every caller writes `try`, no caller can
// ever observe it. **That argument was right and it is why the mark is here
// now**: there is something to report. A hook can fail, its code is recorded
// while the loop is inside C, and this is the one call that is still on the
// stack when the loop comes back. Nothing else can carry it.
//
// The two `#panic`s stay panics and are not folded into the `!`. They are
// reached by a program that imported the core directly and skipped its facade's
// `init`, which is a mistake with no recovery and no caller to report to — the
// panic names the repair. A code for *"you forgot a line"* would be a
// compile-time-shaped problem wearing a run-time answer, and it would sit in the
// same `else` arm as a program's own genuine failures.
//
// **The flag is cleared here and not in `note_failure`'s caller**, which is what
// makes a failing run survivable: a second `run` in the same process starts
// clean. The program's own `quit()` deliberately does not — see the stopping
// section above.
!void run():
    if !g_backend_set:
        #panic("app.run: no backend installed — call a facade's init() first (app:graphical.init, app:headless.init)")
    if g_backend.run == nil:
        #panic("app.run: the installed backend has no run — a Backend's `run` is the loop and may not be nil")
    g_failed = false
    g_fail_code = 0
    g_running = true
    g_backend.run()
    g_running = false
    if g_failed:
        fail g_fail_code
